WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:03.300
It's Michelle of Lab Muffin Beauty 
Science here again with more skin care

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:07.020
related chemistry nerdery. Today we're
going to be discussing a misconception

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:09.330
that's quite common in skincare
communities.

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:14.070
It's about fatty acids and oils. Ihis
might sound really abstract and

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:18.240
theoretical but it's fundamental to
understanding why some claims about oils

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:23.760
aren't as valid as they sound. For
example you might see lauric acid is 15

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:28.740
times better at killing acne bacteria
than benzoyl peroxide. Coconut oil is 50

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:33.090
percent lauric acid, so coconut oil is
going to be fantastic at killing acne

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:38.430
bacteria. Or oleic acid disrupts your
skin barrier. Since argan oil contains

00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:43.980
about 50% oleic acid it will disrupt
your skin. But chemistry and biology say

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:50.030
otherwise. Let's chat about fatty acids
and triglycerides.

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:56.960
An acid in organic chemistry is a group
that contains a carbon double bonded to

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:02.180
an oxygen atom and an OH group. This
hydrogen can ionise which is what makes

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:06.200
it an acid. Fatty acids have this acid
group connected to a long non-polar

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:10.250
hydrogen and carbon chain. This chain
makes the chemical feel greasy and

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:15.290
fatty, hence the name fatty acid. There
are a range of fatty acids. Yhere are

00:01:15.290 --> 00:01:19.070
saturated fatty acids which contain no
double bonds like lauric, myristic

00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:24.410
stearic and palmitic acids. There are
monounsaturated fatty acids that have one

00:01:24.410 --> 00:01:29.090
double bond like oleic and sapienic
acid, and polyunsaturated fatty acids

00:01:29.090 --> 00:01:33.380
that have multiple double bonds like
linoleic and linolenic acid. Some

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:38.030
unsaturated fatty acids are called omega
fatty acids. The omega refers to where

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:40.900
the double bonds are.

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:49.270
A triglyceride also known as a 
triacylglycerol looks like this. It has one

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:53.200
glycerin molecule connected to three
fatty acids via what are called ester

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:57.400
linkages. Triglycerides are the main
components of the common fats and oils

00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:01.420
that you'll know: the vast majority of
plant oils like olive oil, sunflower oil,

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:05.350
coconut oil, but also fats like beef fat
and lard.

00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:10.149
If you zoom into the ester linkages in a
triglyceride you'll notice that the COOH

00:02:10.149 --> 00:02:14.740
acid groups aren't there anymore. There
technically aren't any fatty acids in

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:18.280
the triglyceride at all! That's because
the acids have undergone a chemical

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:22.750
reaction to make the triglyceride so
they're no longer acids. So what happens

00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:31.750
is this OH on the fatty acid comes off
and a hydrogen on the glycerin also

00:02:31.750 --> 00:02:38.980
comes off, and they connect like that. So
you can see the acid no long has that H+

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:43.060
that can come off, and so it's no longer
an acid. It's a little bit like how

00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:46.930
sodium metal and green chlorine gas
undergo a chemical reaction to give

00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:51.370
sodium chloride, which is white and salt
and delicious, not green or metallic or

00:02:51.370 --> 00:02:54.820
gas-like at all.
But chemical reactions can be reversed

00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:58.630
under the right conditions. That's why
you'll see lots of nutritional advice

00:02:58.630 --> 00:03:03.220
saying that fish oil is a good source of
essential omega-3 fatty acids, even

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:06.269
though the actual fish oil contains only
triglycerides.

00:03:06.269 --> 00:03:11.470
Lipase enzymes from your saliva, stomach
and pancreas break up the triglycerides

00:03:11.470 --> 00:03:16.450
and turn them back into free fatty acids.
This breakdown reaction is called

00:03:16.450 --> 00:03:21.250
hydrolysis. Soap makers also talk about
oils in terms of the percentages of

00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:25.390
fatty acids contained in them, because
the main step in soap making involves

00:03:25.390 --> 00:03:29.530
performing a chemical reaction with lye
to break up the covalent bonds in the

00:03:29.530 --> 00:03:34.810
triglycerides to free the fatty acids.
But plant oils contain very little free

00:03:34.810 --> 00:03:40.570
fatty acids. In general you'll have less
than 1% free fatty acids in the oil that

00:03:40.570 --> 00:03:45.070
haven't been either transformed into the
triglyceride or have broken off the

00:03:45.070 --> 00:03:50.230
triglyceride. Too many free fatty acids
actually makes the oil rancid which is

00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:54.160
when it smells and tastes off. For
example coconut

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:59.110
oil can't be sold if it contains more
than 0.5% free fatty acids. Virgin

00:03:59.110 --> 00:04:04.060
coconut oil has an average of 0.1
percent free fatty acids. Cold-pressed

00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:09.760
unrefined rose hip oil has about 2% free
fatty acids. So when we put oils on our

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:15.340
faces we're applying very little free
fatty acids. So the question is: do we

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:18.820
have lipases on the surface of our
skin that can break up these

00:04:18.820 --> 00:04:24.010
triglycerides? If yes then using oils is
the same as using the free fatty acids,

00:04:24.010 --> 00:04:28.540
but if not then it's like going to a
restaurant and ordering a steak and

00:04:28.540 --> 00:04:33.370
being given the steak while it's still
attached to the cow. It turns out that

00:04:33.370 --> 00:04:36.940
the answer is quite complicated.
I've actually been wondering about this

00:04:36.940 --> 00:04:40.750
and reading about it on and off for
about three years, and I've been talking

00:04:40.750 --> 00:04:44.140
to my nerd friend/cosmetic
chemist Stephen about it a few times

00:04:44.140 --> 00:04:48.820
and I finally sat down and read through
hundreds of references before coming to

00:04:48.820 --> 00:04:52.810
a conclusion that I'm somewhat confident
with. I've included the most relevant

00:04:52.810 --> 00:04:56.170
references here, but if you're really
interested in the science and the

00:04:56.170 --> 00:04:59.830
chemistry behind it all I'd encourage
you to do a proper literature review

00:04:59.830 --> 00:05:01.950
yourself.

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:08.110
There are enzymes called lipases in
your skin that can break up

00:05:08.110 --> 00:05:12.900
triglycerides. Some of these are made by
humans but a ton of these are made by

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:17.350
microorganisms living in your skin.
Most of these live under the surface of

00:05:17.350 --> 00:05:21.850
your skin in your pores. Humans produce
an oil called sebum in the sebaceous

00:05:21.850 --> 00:05:25.660
glands under the skin. When it's at the
bottom of the pore near the sebaceous

00:05:25.660 --> 00:05:28.210
gland it contains a high amount of
triglycerides

00:05:28.210 --> 00:05:32.320
and almost no free fatty acids, but by
the time it gets to the skin's surface

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:38.650
around 1/4 of this has broken up to give
15% free fatty acids. Scientists in the

00:05:38.650 --> 00:05:44.110
70s did some experiments and found that
P acnes, the acne bacteria, is the main

00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:47.560
microbe that produces these lipase
enzymes that break down the

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:52.600
triglycerides, but other species can
produce lipase enzymes too. For example

00:05:52.600 --> 00:05:58.060
Malassezia fungus, staph and corynebacterium.
The vast majority of these

00:05:58.060 --> 00:06:03.010
organisms and their lipase enzymes sit
inside the pores. There's also a lipase

00:06:03.010 --> 00:06:06.520
that your body makes in the living
epidermis of your skin that then gets

00:06:06.520 --> 00:06:14.530
released into the stratum corneum with
the rest of the lipids. So there are

00:06:14.530 --> 00:06:18.340
lipases and theoretically they can break
down the triglycerides to give free

00:06:18.340 --> 00:06:23.050
fatty acids - but how much and how fast?
There aren't that many studies that we

00:06:23.050 --> 00:06:26.800
can look at to see how much and how fast
triglycerides get broken down to give

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:30.580
the free fatty acids when they're
applied to the surface of your skin, so

00:06:30.580 --> 00:06:35.080
here's what I've managed to find. In the
1950s there was a radioactivity study

00:06:35.080 --> 00:06:39.190
where radioactive tripalmitin
which is the triglyceride of palmitic

00:06:39.190 --> 00:06:43.510
acid was applied to someone's back
for two and a half hours then wiped off

00:06:43.510 --> 00:06:48.460
and analysed. 6% of the radioactive
tripalmitin was broken up versus

00:06:48.460 --> 00:06:53.380
approximately 30% of the triglycerides
in regular sebum. So why was this figure

00:06:53.380 --> 00:06:57.610
so low? The scientists who did this
experiment suggested that it was likely

00:06:57.610 --> 00:07:01.720
that the oil on the surface couldn't get
into the pore whether lipase enzymes

00:07:01.720 --> 00:07:05.710
were, and even if it did it would
probably get pushed out by the new sebum

00:07:05.710 --> 00:07:10.060
that was coming out of the sebaceous
glands. 6% in 2.5 hours sounds like

00:07:10.060 --> 00:07:13.450
quite a lot especially when you think
about how long oil stays on your skin

00:07:13.450 --> 00:07:17.060
overnight,
but only a really tiny amount of the

00:07:17.060 --> 00:07:21.919
tripalmitin was used in this experiment. It
was 0.5 milligrams which converts to

00:07:21.919 --> 00:07:27.500
0.56 microliters. One drop of
water is about 50 microliters so this is

00:07:27.500 --> 00:07:31.789
one hundredth of a drop, which was
applied over 300 square centimeters

00:07:31.789 --> 00:07:37.400
which is about this much skin. If you use
more oil then it's even less likely that

00:07:37.400 --> 00:07:41.509
it'll get into the pore and so you'll
have an even lower percentage. Another

00:07:41.509 --> 00:07:46.130
study used mixtures of oleic acid as
the free fatty acid and glycerol

00:07:46.130 --> 00:07:51.259
trioleate which is 3 oleic acid
molecules bound up, bonded to glycerin.

00:07:51.259 --> 00:07:56.360
Oleic acid as a free fatty acid is known
to be a barrier disruptor. They made a

00:07:56.360 --> 00:08:00.259
bunch of mixtures that were 0%
free fatty acid and 100 percent

00:08:00.259 --> 00:08:06.800
triglyceride; 25 percent free fatty acid;
50 percent free fatty acid and 100

00:08:06.800 --> 00:08:11.180
percent free fatty acid. They applied one
drop of each mixture to the forearms of

00:08:11.180 --> 00:08:15.020
12 people, covered it with gauze,
gave them plastic wrap so they could

00:08:15.020 --> 00:08:18.770
cover it while they were showering, then
looked at the sites the next day. First

00:08:18.770 --> 00:08:23.570
they measured the trans epidermal water
loss or TEWL which is the leakiness of

00:08:23.570 --> 00:08:28.130
the skin to water leaving. They found
that the sites with 100% triglyceride

00:08:28.130 --> 00:08:32.570
had no change from the untreated areas
of skin. The trans epidermal water loss

00:08:32.570 --> 00:08:36.620
for the rest of the sites was directly
proportional to the amount of free fatty

00:08:36.620 --> 00:08:40.760
acid oleic acid. The scientists also
measured how easily chemicals could

00:08:40.760 --> 00:08:44.540
penetrate the skin going in using
fluorescein dye. The depth of the

00:08:44.540 --> 00:08:48.829
penetration of the dye was proportional
to the amount of oleic acid as well. So

00:08:48.829 --> 00:08:53.390
these results suggest that only very
negligible tiny amounts of triglyceride

00:08:53.390 --> 00:08:57.860
were actually broken up into the free
oleic acid or at least anything that

00:08:57.860 --> 00:09:01.610
was broken down to give oleic acid was
immediately turned into something else

00:09:01.610 --> 00:09:05.630
that didn't disrupt the skin barrier, or
perhaps they just didn't end up in the

00:09:05.630 --> 00:09:10.430
same place. Applying free oleic acid and
applying oleic acid bound up in

00:09:10.430 --> 00:09:15.320
triglycerides definitely did not have
the same effect, at least within the time

00:09:15.320 --> 00:09:19.790
frame of the experiment which was 24
hours. There was a third study which was

00:09:19.790 --> 00:09:24.380
a longer-term study. In this study they
made a cream which contained linoleic

00:09:24.380 --> 00:09:28.100
acid-rich oils which were sunflower and
borage seed oil.

00:09:28.100 --> 00:09:32.449
There was also a plain placebo cream and
both of these cranes were applied to dry

00:09:32.449 --> 00:09:37.339
skin in winter. They were applied twice
daily for four weeks on the inside forearm

00:09:37.339 --> 00:09:42.170
of eleven different people. Each person
applied the active cream to one arm and

00:09:42.170 --> 00:09:47.480
the placebo plain cream to the other arm,
then afterwards the scientists analysed

00:09:47.480 --> 00:09:51.889
the lipids in the stratum corneum. They
looked at the ceramides, free fatty acids

00:09:51.889 --> 00:09:56.269
and cholesterol content after four weeks.
They found that there was actually less

00:09:56.269 --> 00:10:00.709
free fatty acids in the treated area
compared to the plain cream but they

00:10:00.709 --> 00:10:04.550
found that the levels of ceramide 1
linoleate which are ceramides with

00:10:04.550 --> 00:10:08.360
linoleic acid attached were nearly
doubled on the treated part compared to

00:10:08.360 --> 00:10:12.800
the plain cream, while the same type of
ceramide but with oleic acid attached

00:10:12.800 --> 00:10:17.420
was much lower. It's also worth noting
that in sebum in the free fatty acid

00:10:17.420 --> 00:10:21.740
portion there's actually less
unsaturated acids like linoleic acid

00:10:21.740 --> 00:10:26.630
and oleic acid than in the original
triglycerides. So what this suggests is

00:10:26.630 --> 00:10:31.819
that some free fatty acids, linoleic acid
and oleic acid, are selectively removed

00:10:31.819 --> 00:10:35.839
after triglycerides are hydrolyzed or
broken down, and used to make other

00:10:35.839 --> 00:10:41.079
substances like ceramides.

00:10:41.079 --> 00:10:46.160
So what does this mean? From these
studies we can see that triglycerides

00:10:46.160 --> 00:10:50.120
applied on skin aren't broken down to
give you free fatty acids on your skin.

00:10:50.120 --> 00:10:54.290
This is important because in
pharmacology the dose is very important.

00:10:54.290 --> 00:10:58.820
For example if you take one ibuprofen
painkiller tablet it's less effective

00:10:58.820 --> 00:11:03.140
than taking two. Taking one ibuprofen
pain tablet might get rid of your

00:11:03.140 --> 00:11:07.190
headache, but taking tiny portions of one
tablet over three days won't be

00:11:07.190 --> 00:11:11.690
noticeable. It's even more complicated
because breaking down the triglycerides

00:11:11.690 --> 00:11:16.070
depends on microorganisms and different
people have different microorganisms on

00:11:16.070 --> 00:11:20.570
their skin. For example men have more
microbes on their skin than women and

00:11:20.570 --> 00:11:25.040
other factors like climate and genetics
will make a big difference too. Different

00:11:25.040 --> 00:11:29.360
parts of your face and body also have
different microorganisms. One study found

00:11:29.360 --> 00:11:32.750
that sebum triglycerides were
hydrolysed more quickly at the nose than

00:11:32.750 --> 00:11:36.920
on the forehead. Taking antibiotics will
change your skin's microbes obviously,

00:11:36.920 --> 00:11:40.220
but so will other things like using
different cleansers, creams and

00:11:40.220 --> 00:11:45.079
antibacterial products. So in conclusion
we shouldn't rely on oils for the

00:11:45.079 --> 00:11:48.380
properties of their free fatty acids,
unless they've been shown to be

00:11:48.380 --> 00:11:52.430
effective at very low concentrations,
although having them in an oil and

00:11:52.430 --> 00:11:55.880
having them in a solution of alcohol,
which is how they usually test the

00:11:55.880 --> 00:11:59.930
activity of these free fatty acids, will
have very different effects on how well

00:11:59.930 --> 00:12:03.740
they penetrate your skin. But this
doesn't mean that all oils are useless.

00:12:03.740 --> 00:12:08.540
They can still be very beneficial for
example oils are often excellent

00:12:08.540 --> 00:12:12.500
moisturizers. They can be occlusive and
slow down how quickly water leaves your

00:12:12.500 --> 00:12:16.930
skin and make it dehydrated, plus they
work as emollients that soften your skin.

00:12:16.930 --> 00:12:21.649
They are also very good bases for oil
soluble ingredients and sometimes they

00:12:21.649 --> 00:12:26.149
naturally have beneficial ingredients in
them. For example rosehip oil contains a

00:12:26.149 --> 00:12:30.709
few forms of vitamin A mostly beta
carotene but there's also a tiny amount

00:12:30.709 --> 00:12:35.329
of retinoic acid or tretinoin in there
as well. They can also potentially be

00:12:35.329 --> 00:12:39.560
good sources of fatty acids for making
ceramides in your skin as shown in the

00:12:39.560 --> 00:12:45.170
study on sunflower and borage seed oils. So
in summary oils contain fatty acids

00:12:45.170 --> 00:12:50.959
bound up in triglycerides, not free fatty
acids. Applying skin care oils won't give

00:12:50.959 --> 00:12:54.710
you the same effect
as applying the free fatty acids. That's

00:12:54.710 --> 00:12:58.670
all from me today! If you like my videos
I'd love it if you could subscribe to my

00:12:58.670 --> 00:13:02.750
channel and like my videos. I also have a
blog where I have lots and lots of

00:13:02.750 --> 00:13:06.980
beauty science. You can also follow me on
Facebook and Instagram where I post lots

00:13:06.980 --> 00:13:17.340
and lots of skincare tips based on
science. See you next time! :)

